Appleseed patch series
======================

Upstream: https://github.com/appleseedhq/appleseed
Base: c4ba82893febd711befb194f905e955d3ee143a3
Former fork: e85844681afba97a8d9ff72b444c7d05230ac346

Apply bootstrap, boost, and art groups in the order declared in
Series.cmake. Each numbered patch is a consolidated review topic.
HISTORY preserves all 205 original downstream commit messages, authors,
dates, and parent IDs, including the earlier fork merges.

The bootstrap group establishes dependency and platform compatibility,
including shared-library exports used by basic framebuffer display.
The bootstrap plus boost groups form a separately buildable core/CLI
subset. Apply patches 01 through 17 to remove core Boost requirements
without taking the art and rendering enhancements. Configure bext with
-DAPPLESEED_PATCH_STAGE=CORE to select that prefix; the default ART
selects the complete series. Invalid stage names are rejected.
The final core topic
stages the shader resources needed to compile and test against modern
OSL. Disable both optional Python bindings; they continue to require
Boost.Python. The core uses PCRE2 for Perl-style filters, preserving their
established syntax.

The art group adds procedural geometry, material and shader contracts,
resource/font updates, optical transport, emitters, volumes, and ray
differentials. These patches are ordered cumulatively; their numbering
does not imply that every art topic is independent of earlier topics.

The core prefix does not include the renderer APIs used by the enhanced
BRL-CAD art implementation. Pair current art with the complete series.
The original art sources used Boost only through their build rules;
removing those discovery, include, link, and definition requirements is
a separate compatibility change from the later art features. The core
qualification covers Appleseed itself, not a rebuilt original art.

The complete series reproduces the former fork plus the two existing
bext adapters exactly, including file modes. Those adapters now belong
to the bootstrap group. The original brlcad branch remains available
as historical reference; builds use the unchanged upstream base.

Review topics
-------------

01. bootstrap: Dependency modules
02. bootstrap: Native build targets
03. bootstrap: Library api compatibility
04. bootstrap: Shader api compatibility
05. bootstrap: Support files
06. bootstrap: Support bundled libraries and platform toolchains
07. bootstrap: Harden core helpers and export image conversion
08. boost: Use standard optional caches and drop unused Wave
09. boost: Use standard containers for the LRU cache index
10. boost: Use a read-only standard shader surface-area index
11. boost: Use C++17 filesystem and native file timestamps
12. boost: Use native atomics and standard renderer counters
13. boost: Use standard locks, threads and condition variables
14. boost: Use chrono timestamps and remove DateTime adapters
15. boost: Remove Bind and synchronize denoiser workers
16. boost: Use standard helpers and PCRE2 without core Boost
17. boost: Stage complete shader resources and executable tools
18. art: Preserve procedural hit data, derivatives and ownership
19. art: Validate OSL inputs and align query search policy
20. art: Preserve bump frames and correct texture decoding
21. art: Replace vendored font code with bounded struetype
22. art: Correct ideal glass and ray differential sampling
23. art: Correct OSL mixtures and plastic material sampling
24. art: Correct coat interfaces and expose the glossy layer
25. art: Correct panorama filtering, depth and image helpers
26. art: Honor cancellation during samples and job waits
27. art: Support orthographic contours through translucent covers
28. art: Initialize media and correct glass transport scaling
29. art: Resolve coincident optical state before shading
30. art: Preserve physical origins through procedural boundaries
31. art: Keep trace messages current and expose typed object data
32. art: Preserve sampled emitter attributes, transforms and sides
33. art: Preserve hierarchical emitter geometry and ray parameters
34. art: Propagate procedural emitter weights and densities
35. art: Use physical origins and finite guides for emitter MIS
36. art: Integrate participating media from physical path origins
37. art: Keep image channel names and color interpretations valid
38. art: Stabilize thin-media sampling and panorama densities
39. art: Integrate environment light through hidden boundaries
40. art: Evaluate directional emission in its optical context
41. art: Resolve real surfaces behind invisible covers
42. art: Propagate selected normals through specular closures
43. art: Differentiate GGX visible-normal sampled paths
44. art: Differentiate native and OSL surface frames
45. art: Stabilize grazing and normal-incidence sampling
46. art: Wire standard OSL microfacet reflection and frames
47. art: Resolve closure indices in their adjacent media
48. art: Support standard OSL microfacet transmission
49. art: Keep OSL emission normalization finite
50. art: Recover height curvature with isolated bump prefixes
